  { q: 'Why is Odin called the Raven-God?', a: 'Odin is called the Raven-God because his ravens Huginn and Muninn — Thought and Memory — fly throughout the world each day and report back to him, giving him knowledge of all events. This knowledge-gathering function defines Odin\'s character: he is the god of wisdom, strategy, poetry, and death — all obtained through seeking knowledge at great cost. The raven, as his primary instrument of knowledge, defines him. Additionally, Odin\'s association with battle and the dead connects him to the battlefield raven as a scavenger of the slain.' },
