    <h1>Odin's Raven Names: Hrafnaguð and the Raven Epithets of the Allfather</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Among Odin's 200+ attested names, several specifically reference his identity as the Raven-God: <em>Hrafnaguð</em> and <em>Hrafnáss</em> ("Raven-God") are the most explicit. His ravens Huginn and Muninn are so central to his identity that he is kenned as their master throughout skaldic poetry. These raven names encode Odin's nature as a deity of knowledge, death, and the battlefield — all domains where the raven is his instrument.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven Names</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The following Odin names directly reference ravens:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hrafnaguð</strong> — "Raven-God." The most direct of the raven epithets. Used in skaldic poetry to identify Odin as the deity specifically associated with the raven. Composed of <em>hrafn</em> (raven) + <em>guð</em> (god).</li>
      <li><strong>Hrafnáss</strong> — "Raven-God" / "Raven Deity." An alternative compound using <em>áss</em> (a divine being of the Æsir) rather than <em>guð</em>. Same meaning as Hrafnaguð.</li>
      <li><strong>Farmaguð</strong> and similar "travelling god" names — while not exclusively raven-linked, Odin's travelling identity relates to his ravens' daily flights across the world.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Beyond explicit raven names, Odin is extensively kenned in relation to Huginn and Muninn in skaldic poetry — called "the feeder of Huginn," "lord of the ravens," "master of Muninn," and similar constructions. These kennings effectively function as additional raven-linked names.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What the Raven Names Reveal About Odin</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The identification of Odin as Hrafnaguð places the raven at the centre of his divine identity. This is unusual: most Norse deities are not so closely identified with a single animal. Thor is not "the eagle-god" or "the wolf-god" despite his mythology; Odin's identification as specifically the raven-god suggests the ravens are not merely symbolic accessories but essential expressions of his nature.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Three aspects of Odin converge in the raven name:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Omniscience:</strong> his ravens gather knowledge from across the nine worlds, making Odin the best-informed being in existence — the essence of his wisdom.</li>
      <li><strong>Death:</strong> ravens are battlefield scavengers; Odin is the lord of the slain, the master of Valhöll. The raven on the battlefield is Odin's presence at the moment of death.</li>
      <li><strong>Magic:</strong> in some traditions, Odin practices <em>seiðr</em> (a shamanic magic form) which involves sending out the consciousness in animal form. His ravens are externalised consciousness — literally Huginn (Thought) and Muninn (Memory) flying free of his body.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Odin's Names in the Eddas</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The Poetic Edda's <em>Grímnismál</em> includes a section where Odin (in disguise as Grímnir) recites his own names — a list that fills several stanzas. The same poem contains the naming of Huginn and Muninn (stanza 20). The juxtaposition — the naming of the ravens immediately adjacent to the recitation of his divine names — signals their inseparability.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The Prose Edda's <em>Gylfaginning</em> includes a longer catalogue of Odin names compiled by Snorri Sturluson from earlier tradition. Snorri glosses the raven names as reflecting Odin's ownership of the ravens and their role in his information network.</p>
  </section>

